ean","termine":"*dʰeygʷ-","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"ine-pro","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Borrowed from English fix, from Middle English fixen, borrowed from Old French *fixer, from fixe (“fastened; fixed”), from Latin fīxus (“immovable; steady; stable; fixed”), from fīgere (“to drive in; stick; fasten”), from Proto-Indo-European *dʰeygʷ- (“to jab; stick; set”). Doublet of fixen and fixe"},{"parola":"file","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":2,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"no","significato":"file (an aggregation of data on a storage device identified by a name)","termine_origine":"file","catena":[{"codice":"en","nome":"English","termine":"file","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"fro","nome":"Old French","termine":"fil","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"filum","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"la","ambiti":["scienza","informatica"],"via":"","testo":"From English file (“computer file”), from Old French fil (“thread”), from Latin filum (“thread”). Related to fileren (“to fillet”) and file (“queue, traffic jam”)."},{"parola":"gijn","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"prestito","incerta":"no","significato":"a multiple-pulley block with three cheeks","termine_origine":"gin","catena":[{"codice":"en","nome":"English","termine":"gin","categoria":"prestito"},{"codice":"fro","nome":"Old French","termine":"engin","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"ingenium","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"la","ambiti":["mare"],"via":"","testo":"Borrowed from English gin, ultimately from Old French engin, from Latin ingenium."},{"parola":"humor","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"prestito","incerta":"no","significato":"humour (sense of amusement)","termine_origine":"humor","catena":[{"codice":"en","nome":"English","termine":"humor","categoria":"prestito"},{"codice":"fro","nome":"Old French","termine":"humor","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"hūmor","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"la","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Borrowed from English humor (US), from Old French humor (“bodily fluid”), from Latin hūmor. Doublet of humeur (“mood, mental state”). The meaning of humor as in \"a sense of amusement\" entered Dutch from the US spelling of humour around ~1839."},{"parola":"jekker","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"prestito","incerta":"no","significato":"jacket, short coat, jerkin","termine_origine":"jacket","catena":[{"codice":"en","nome":"English","termine":"jacket","categoria":"prestito"},{"codice":"fro","nome":"Old French","termine":"jaquet","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"fro","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Considered to be a derivative of jak (“upper body garment”); it could instead be a borrowing from English jacket, which would explain the initial -e-. Both are ultimately from Old French jaquet, diminutive of jaque (“tight-fitting garment”)."},{"parola":"renderen","pos":"verb","n_etimologia":2,"categoria":"prestito","incerta":"no","significato":"to render (of digital media)","termine_origine":"render","catena":[{"codice":"en","nome":"English","termine":"render","categoria":"prestito"},{"codice":"fro","nome":"Old French","termine":"rendre","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"fro","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"From English render, from Middle English renderen, from Old French rendre, from which Etymology 1 above also ultimately derives."},{"parola":"script","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"prestito","incerta":"no","significato":"script (written text of a dramatic performance)","termine_origine":"script","catena":[{"codice":"en","nome":"English","termine":"script","categoria":"prestito"},{"codice":"fro","nome":"Old French","termine":"escrit","categoria":"prestito"},{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"scrīptum","categoria":"ereditato"},{"codice":"itc-pro","nome":"Proto-Italic","termine":"*skreiβō","categoria":"ereditato"},{"codice":"ine-pro","nome":"Proto-In